<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol invasions of Anatolia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> were the campaigns by which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> subdued the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> in the mid-13th century. A Mongol army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a> at Köse Dağ in 1243, making the sultanate a tributary, and Baiju defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> again in 1256. Anatolia then came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, whose decline in the early 14th century allowed Turkish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, to rise.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-invasions-of-anatolia.jpg" alt="Mongol invasions of Anatolia" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qiushufang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_invasions_of_Anatolia_1231-1232,_1242-1243.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 1230, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chormaqan">Chormaqan</a> as its military governor, there were at first no hostilities with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a> and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a> swore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> to the Great Khan Ögedei and paid at least a token tribute. When Ögedei died in 1241, Kaykhusraw repudiated his vassalage. Chormaqan's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> summoned him to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> in person, give hostages and accept a Mongol darughachi; when the sultan refused, Baiju declared war, and in 1241 his army besieged and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> after its inhabitants insulted his envoy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1243 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> returned with Georgian and Armenian warriors under Avag and Shanshe and stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> with twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a> when its governor Yakut refused to surrender. Kaykhusraw gathered his forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, with help from the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, the Ayyubid sultan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and Frankish mercenaries; his army was larger than the Mongol force. He marched some 320 kilometres to Köse Dağ, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> had arrived in June 1243. After an indecisive first stage and heavy losses he withdrew at night, and Baiju received the submission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Divriği</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> after a short resistance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> the sultan's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> accepted terms of resubmission and a yearly tribute in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and sheep, but almost half of the sultanate stayed under Mongol occupation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hethum-i">Hethum I</a> of the Armenian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> made peace in 1243 and sent his brother Sembat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> in 1247. Real power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> remained with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> until the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> in 1335, and a garrison was stationed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> beyliks did not mint coins in their own leaders' names while under Ilkhanid suzerainty; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a> was the first to do so, in the 1320s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mongol conquest of Anatolia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
